==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|enablen}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|en|able}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈneɪbəl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-enable.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪbəl|s=3}}

===Verb===
{{wikipedia|enabling}}
{{en-verb}}

# To make somebody able (to do, or to be, something); to give sufficient ability or power to do or to be; to give strength or ability to.
#* '''1611''', ''[[King James Bible]]'', "1 Tim. i. 12"
#*: And I thank Christ Jesus our Lord, who hath '''enabled''' me, for that he counted me faithful, putting me into the ministry; who was before a blasphemer, and a persecutor, and injurious: but I obtained mercy, because I did it ignorantly in unbelief.
#: {{syn|en|empower|endow}}
# To [[affirm]]; to make firm and strong.
# To [[qualify]] or [[approve]] for some role or position; to render sanction or authorization to; to confirm suitability for.
#: {{syn|en|let|permit|authorize}}
# To yield the opportunity or provide the possibility for something; to provide with means, opportunities, and the like.
#: {{syn|en|allow}}
#* '''1711''', October 13, {{w|Joseph Addison}}, ''{{w|The Spectator}}'', number 195
#*: Temperance gives Nature her full play, and '''enables''' her to exert herself in all her force and vigor.
#* '''April 16, 2018''', Norimitsu Onishi and Selam Gebrekidan writing in ''The New York Times'', ''[https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/16/world/africa/south-africa-corruption-jacob-zuma-african-national-congress.html ‘They Eat Money’: How Mandela’s Political Heirs Grow Rich Off Corruption]''
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=72-3|magazine={{w|The Economist}}
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/international/science-and-technology/21580133-how-microbes-promote-liver-cancer-overweight-punch-gut A punch in the gut]
|passage=Mostly, the microbiome is beneficial. It helps with digestion and '''enables''' people to extract a lot more calories from their food than would otherwise be possible. Research over the past few years, however, has implicated it in diseases from atherosclerosis to asthma to autism.}}
#* '''2009''', [[w:Meribeth Dayme|Meribeth A. Dayme]], ''Dynamics of the Singing Voice'', Springer Science & Business Media, p. 174:
#*: Trainers of modern athletes monitor performance by using high tech equipment and biometric bodysuits with embedded sensors to '''enable''' detailed analysis of movement, balance, efficiency for athletic performance.
# To imply or tacitly confer excuse for an action or a behavior.
#: {{ux|en|His parents '''enabled''' him to go on buying drugs.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics}} To put a circuit element into action by supplying a suitable input pulse.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|electronics|computing}} To [[activate]], to make operational (especially of a function of an electronic or mechanical device).
#: {{syn|en|activate|turn on}}
#: {{ant|en|disable}}

